WEBVTT

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:04.650
You sit down.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.432
She looks at you for three seconds, and says,

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.183
"You've been carrying something heavy lately."

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:12.700
And she's right.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.245
How could she know?

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.561
Psychologists say it takes no magic.

00:00:18.661 --> 00:00:20.220
Just three tricks.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.881
Trick one: words that fit everyone.

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:31.181
In 1948, a psychologist named Bertram Forer gave his students a personality test.

00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:34.661
A week later, each one got a "personal" result.

00:00:35.261 --> 00:00:37.076
It said things like this:

00:00:37.176 --> 00:00:40.697
"You can be outgoing, but sometimes you need time alone."

00:00:41.797 --> 00:00:45.809
They rated it 4.26 out of 5 for accuracy.

00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:52.169
But every student got the same words, copied from an astrology book.

00:00:54.869 --> 00:00:57.516
But vague words don't explain the hits.

00:00:57.816 --> 00:00:59.716
A good reader watches you.

00:00:59.866 --> 00:01:01.190
Your hands.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.684
How tired you look.

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:05.637
Where you work.

00:01:06.487 --> 00:01:08.173
What time you came in.

00:01:09.373 --> 00:01:11.359
And above all, your question.

00:01:11.459 --> 00:01:14.596
Most people ask about love, work or money.

00:01:15.996 --> 00:01:17.725
Then she tests a guess:

00:01:17.925 --> 00:01:19.327
"I'm seeing a J…"

00:01:19.377 --> 00:01:21.639
If you react, she keeps going.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:23.646
If you don't, she moves on,

00:01:23.696 --> 00:01:25.724
and you fill in the gaps for her.

00:01:25.824 --> 00:01:27.873
This is called cold reading.

00:01:30.073 --> 00:01:34.235
A young palm reader named Ray Hyman once tried an experiment.

00:01:34.335 --> 00:01:38.155
A friend told him to read every palm the opposite way.

00:01:38.255 --> 00:01:40.645
His clients were just as amazed.

00:01:42.945 --> 00:01:45.677
And the third trick is one you do yourself.

00:01:46.177 --> 00:01:49.891
You remember what she got right, and forget what she got wrong.

00:01:51.491 --> 00:01:54.585
By the time you tell a friend, she was amazing.

00:01:55.485 --> 00:01:59.732
Therefore, take away the watching and the talking, and see what's left.

00:01:59.932 --> 00:02:04.875
In 1985, astrologers who never met the people they were reading

00:02:04.925 --> 00:02:06.804
did no better than chance.

00:02:09.704 --> 00:02:11.817
But that isn't the whole story.

00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:19.535
When life feels uncertain, a reading gives you a calm half hour, someone who listens, and words for what you're worried about.

00:02:20.435 --> 00:02:22.932
A good reader is a good listener.

00:02:24.132 --> 00:02:27.973
So next time a reading feels exactly right, ask yourself:

00:02:28.073 --> 00:02:30.336
did the stars know, or did you?

00:02:31.836 --> 00:02:35.635
Try our deck on page 8, and watch yourself make it fit.
